{
  "sections": {
    "opening": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Not in a dramatic way. In the quiet way. The way you agree to things you do not want. The way you feel vaguely unlike yourself in certain rooms. The way you have needs you have never said out loud. The way you wonder sometimes if the version of you that existed before all the accommodating is still in there somewhere.</p><p>She is. This journal is how you find her.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "text"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "background-1",
        "container_type": "container",
        "padding_top": 50,
        "padding_bottom": 30,
        "mobile_padding_top": 35,
        "mobile_padding_bottom": 20
      }
    },
    "whats_happening": {
      "type": "rich-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "What you're carrying has a name. Shadow work brings it into the light.",
            "heading_size": "h1"
          }
        },
        "text": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>Carl Jung identified what he called the shadow \u2014 the parts of ourselves we have disowned, repressed, or hidden because they felt unsafe to express. These are not the dark parts. They are simply the parts that did not fit the role we were expected to play.</p><p>When the shadow is unexamined, it runs the show from underneath. It shapes decisions without your awareness. It drives patterns you cannot explain. It creates a persistent sense of not quite being yourself.</p><p>Shadow work is the structured process of bringing those parts into conscious awareness \u2014 not to judge them, but to integrate them. When the hidden parts of you become visible, they stop driving from the back seat.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading",
        "text"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "color_scheme": "background-2",
        "container_type": "container",
        "padding_top": 40,
        "padding_bottom": 40,
        "mobile_padding_top": 30,
        "mobile_padding_bottom": 30
      }
    },
    "framework": {
      "type": "multicolumn",
      "blocks": {
        "fw_jung": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Jungian Shadow Work",
            "text": "<p>Drawn from the foundational work of Carl Jung and extended by contemporary depth psychologists \u2014 provides a structured methodology for identifying, examining, and integrating the disowned self. This is not free-form writing. It is directed inquiry with a clear destination.</p>",
            "link_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        },
        "fw_neuro": {
          "type": "column",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Neuroplasticity",
            "text": "<p>Provides the mechanism for why this works at the brain level. Bringing unconscious patterns into conscious awareness \u2014 and repeating new, intentional responses \u2014 literally changes neural architecture. The journal is structured to use this mechanism deliberately.</p><p>45 days. Because this work takes longer than 30. And it is worth it.</p>",
            "link_label": "",
            "link": ""
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "fw_jung",
        "fw_neuro"
      ],
      "settings": {
        "title": "Built on Jungian shadow work and neuroplasticity. Not journaling for its own sake.",
        "heading_size": "h1",
        "heading_align": "center",
        "color_scheme": "background-1",
        "container_type": "container",
        "image_width": "full",
        "image_ratio": "adapt",
        "columns_desktop": 2,
        "column_alignment": "left",
        "background_style": "primary",
        "column_padding": "medium",
        "button_label": "",
        "columns_mobile": "1",
